            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm in support of this patch, but I'm not following what the ud is.  dev is a shared_ptr, so what's existing just adds to the refcount, and should be defined, keeping the device alive until it calls back?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Hmm, possibly getting into the weeds here, but because it's a const reference and not by value, the reference increment comes from the on-stack instance of the shared pointer in the caller. The lambda in the implementation captures a reference to that stack object. It's the stack object that's my concern, not the object pointed to by the smart pointer's stored pointer."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> The lambda in the implementation captures a reference to that stack object\n\nThe previous code was\n\n```\n    dev->setup([weak{weak_from_this()},\n                dev](const std::error_code& ec,\n                     const std::shared_ptr<MCTPEndpoint>& ep) mutable {\n```\n\nboth weak and dev are captured by value, not reference, so I don't understand the response.  You can make a copy of a shared/weak pointer by const reference.\n\nAnywho, this patch is fine, I just suspect there's some other lifetime issue somewhere."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "> both weak and dev are captured by value, not reference, so I don't understand the response.\n\nAh, so I think my understanding was a bit too C-brained with treating references like a pointer rather than an alias for the object. Under the latter perspective passing by-value copies the underlying object rather than the \"reference\", which matches what you're describing.\n\nI was trying to eliminate memory corruption as the source of an observation that ended with errors from the sd_bus APIs, but it turned out to be something else entirely. As such I'm not aware of further lifetime issues, but given their elusiveness I won't go so far as to say that there aren't any \ud83d\ude0a\n\nI'll tweak the commit message to remove this bit of the discussion."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/mctp/MCTPReactor.cpp",
                    "line": 90,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Nit, you could consider passing by weak_ptr here, to denote to the caller that this API doesn't take ownership.  Its' somewhat of a nit, so up to you if you want to implement."
                }
            ],
